# XSH atomic_is_lock_free(), and the ATOMIC_*_LOCK_FREE macros
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Whether a type is lock-free is the implementation's business; the page
# and <stdatomic.h> fix only consistency and the meaning of the macros.

assertion  XSH.atomic_is_lock_free.001
anchor     functions/atomic_is_lock_free.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/atomic_is_lock_free.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       For atomic_bool, every atomic integer type and four atomic
           pointer types, atomic_is_lock_free() answers the same (as true or
           false) when called twice on one object, on a second object, on an
           automatic object, through a const volatile pointer, and with a
           null pointer.
test       t/001-consistent.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_is_lock_free.002
anchor     basedefs/stdatomic.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       ATOMIC_BOOL_, CHAR_, CHAR16_T_, CHAR32_T_, WCHAR_T_, SHORT_, INT_,
           LONG_, LLONG_ and POINTER_LOCK_FREE are defined and usable in #if
           with a value of 0, 1 or 2.
note       The macros belong to <stdatomic.h>; they are tested here with
           atomic_is_lock_free() because their meaning is the lock-free
           property it reports.
test       t/002-macros-values.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_is_lock_free.003
anchor     basedefs/stdatomic.h.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atomic_is_lock_free.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       Where a lock-free macro is 2, atomic_is_lock_free() is true for the
           signed and unsigned atomic types and pointer types it covers;
           where it is 0, it is false for them. A macro of 1 is not
           constrained.
note       If every macro is 1 the test reports NOTINUSE (nothing to
           compare).
test       t/003-macros-agree.c
